description:
A moth that I discovered on the wall of a building near the intersection of Ciudadela and Plaza Independencia in the Ciudad Vieja District of Montevideo, Uruguay.  The moth had taken refuge there to escape some showery weather that had rolled in from the southwest.  It was between 3.0 and 3.5 cm long, and between 2.5 and 3.0 cm wide.

I know virtually nothing about South American moths, so I am leaving this one identified to family only.  Further identification to genus or species I will leave as an exercise to any enthusiasts of South American moths who might be lurking out there within the iNaturalist community.

@pablobaldu @mirmeleon

A Monocot of some sort, photographed on public space near the intersection of La Cumparsita and Rambla República Argentina in Montevideo, Uruguay.  iNaturalist suggests Crowpoison (Nothoscordum bivalve), and although the flowers of Crowpoison are similar, they only have a single whorl of tepals.   The flowers in my photograph have two whorls of tepals - an outer whorl that is spreading when the flower is open and an inner row that forms a central "ring" around the flower's pistil and stamens. 

Overall dimensions: The plant itself was about 20 cm tall.  Flower tepals approximately 15 mm long and about 5 mm wide at their widest point.  

@pablobaldu
